seful. Since I need to form four melds, keeping tiles that can form Chows or Pungs is better.\n\nIn my hand, the T4-5-6-7 sequence is promising. If I can form two Chows here, that's two melds. Then maybe another Chow in another suit. For example, in Dots: B1 and B2 could be part of a Chow if I have B3, but I don't. Or maybe in Characters: W1 and W2 could be part of a Chow if I have W3. But I don't. So maybe not.\n\nAlternatively, if I can form a Pung of F2, that would be good. But I need another F2. There are 3 left. If I discard F2, I lose the chance to form a Pung. But since I have only one, maybe it's better to keep it.\n\nSo, the best tiles to discard are those not part of any potential meld. W6 and W8 are single tiles. Let's check the unshown counts for those. W6 has 1 left, W8 has 2 left. If I discard W6, there's only one left, so maybe others have it. Discarding W6 might be safe. Similarly for W8.\n\nBetween W6 and W8, which is better to discard? If I discard W6, maybe another player can Gang if they have three W6. But since there's only one left, they might not. Alternatively, if I discard W8, same thing. But since I have W8 in my hand, maybe it's better to keep it for a possible Chow if I can get W7 and W9. But I don't have those. Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, maybe discard T9. T9 is a terminal. If I discard it, it's safe. But if I need it for a pair or terminal-based hand, maybe keep. But I have T9 once. Not helpful for a pair unless I draw another.\n\nAnother angle: My hand has 14 tiles. To win, I need four melds and a pair. Let's see if I can form them now or with a draw.\n\nPossible melds:\n\n- T4-5-6 (Chow)\n- T5-6-7 (Chow)\n- Maybe another Chow in another suit. Let's see: W1-2-3 (if I have W3, which I don't). Or W4-5-6 (if I have W5). I have W4 and W6 but no W5. Or W6-7-8 (if I have W7). I have W6 and W8 but no W7. So not helpful.\n\nAlternatively, maybe a Pung of B1? No, only one. Or B2? Only one. Or F2? Only one. So maybe not.\n\nSo with two Chows in 