on 10: Play W1. I have W1 and W2. If I discard W1, maybe someone can take it for a Chi. But I need to see if keeping W1 helps in forming a meld.\n\nNow, considering my current hand and the melds, I need to maximize my chances of completing a winning hand. Let's see what possible winning hands I can have.\n\nIf I can form more melds and pairs. Let's see:\n\nCurrent melds: [T3, T4, T5].\n\nHand tiles: W1, W2, W4, T6, T7, T9, T9, B2, B9, F2, F3.\n\nPossible melds:\n\n- W1, W2, W3 (Chi) \u2013 need W3. If I have W3, that's a Chi. But I don't have it.\n\n- T6, T7, T8 (Chi) \u2013 need T8.\n\n- B2, B3, B4 (Chi) \u2013 need B3 and B4. But I have B2 and B9. Not helpful.\n\n- F2, F2, F2 (Pung) \u2013 need two more F2s. I have one, so if I can get two more, but unlikely.\n\n- T9, T9 (Pair).\n\nOther possibilities:\n\nIf I can form two more melds and a pair. For example, if I have a pair of T9s, and two more melds.\n\nIf I can form a Chi of W1, W2, W3 and a Chi of T6, T7, T8, then with the existing Chi of T3-T5, that would give three melds and a pair of T9s. That would be a winning hand.\n\nSo to do that, I need W3 and T8. Are those available?\n\nLooking at unshown tiles:\n\nW3: 1 remaining.\n\nT8: 4 remaining.\n\nSo if I can keep waiting for W3 and T8, but I need to discard tiles to make space.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can form a different set of melds. Let's see.\n\nAnother option: If I can form a Pung of F2 (my seat wind), but I only have one. So not helpful.\n\nLooking at the hand again. If I discard B2, which is a single tile, maybe that's the best move. Let me check the unshown tiles for B2: B2 has 1 remaining. So if I discard B2, there's a chance someone else might take it, but since I have a Chi of T3-T5, and B2 is a single, it's better to discard it.\n\nIf I discard B2, then my hand becomes [W1, W2, W4, T6, T7, T9, T9, B9, F2, F3]. Now, I need to form two more melds and a pair.\n\nPossible melds:\n\n- W1, W2, W3 (Chi) \u2013 need W3.\n\n- T6, T7, T8 (Chi) \u2013 need T8.\n\n- T9, T9 (Pair).\n\nSo same as before. Discarding B2 frees up a tile to get W3 or T8.\n\nAlternatively, if I discard T6 or T7, maybe to get T8 or T3, but T3 is already in the meld.\n\nAnother thought: The player across has a meld of [B5, B6, B7], and the previous player has [B6, B7, B8]. So B6 and B7 are common in melds. If I have B9, maybe someone is waiting for B8. But I have B9, which is a terminal. Not sure.\n\nLooking at the valid actions, the best move seems to be to discard B2, as it's a single tile and not part of any potential meld. By discarding B2, I can keep W1, W2, W4 in hand, which could form a Chi if I get W3, and T6, T7 which could form a Chi if I get T8. The pair of T9s is already there. So this would give me a chance to complete two more melds and the pair, leading to a winning hand.\n\nAnother option is to discard T7. If I discard T7, I have T6 left. If I can get T8, then T6, T7, T8. But since I discarded T7, someone else might take it. However, T8 is still available. But keeping T7 might be better for forming the Chi.\n\nBetween discarding B2 and T7. Since B2 is not part of any potential meld, and T7 is part of a possible Chi, maybe it's better to keep T7 and discard B2.\n\nSo the best action is Play B2.\n", "output": "Evaluation: [The best move is to discard B2, as it is a single tile not part of any potential melds, freeing up space to form additional melds like W1-W2-W3 or T6-T7-T8. This increases the chance of completing the hand with the existing pair of T9s.]\nReason: [Discarding B2 allows the player to retain key tiles (W1, W2, T6, T7) that can form two additional chows if W3 or T8 are drawn, while maintaining the pair of T9s. This strategy maximizes the potential to complete a winning hand with three melds and a pair.]\nAnswer: Play B2"}
